export const DEMO_COURSES = ["---\ntitle: \"Junior Electrical Engineer Path — From First Principles to Motor, Generator & Critical Evaluation\"\npurpose: \"Reach junior-level practical and theoretical competence in electricity and basic electronics. Understand the underlying principles deeply enough to build real devices (including a motor that also generates) and to critically evaluate claims about free or zero-point energy against established physics.\"\naudience: \"Motivated adults with no prior formal training who have a real question or project they care about. Assumes zero knowledge. Designed so it can later be offered to others.\"\noutcome: \"A working motor that can also act as a generator, a clear written evaluation of free-energy / zero-point claims grounded in the principles learned, and the ability to explain the core ideas from first principles to another person.\"\nlicense: \"CC-BY-SA 4.0\"\nauthor: \"Sand Pavilion (built with the Theoretical Minimum method and the 7-step Course Authoring protocol)\"\nsource: \"Prepared for Sand Pavilion, August 2026. Core materials drawn from open resources (Lessons in Electric Circuits, Ellingson Electromagnetics, Falstad/CircuitJS) and standard practical references.\"\nprerequisites:\n  - \"A genuine personal question or project that makes the work worth doing\"\n  - \"Willingness to do at least one focused thing most days\"\n  - \"Pen, paper, and access to a browser for free simulation tools\"\ncategory: \"Skill\"\ntrack: \"Electrical Engineering / Practical Physics\"\nlevel: 201\n---\n\n# How to walk this course\n\nThis is not a requirement. It is a privilege.\n\nYou are choosing to pursue something difficult because it matters to you. The only entrance condition is an honest answer to one question:\n\n**What is the real question or dream that brings you here?**\n\nFor some it is \"Can we give energy out for free?\"\nFor others it is \"I want to understand how the machines around me actually work\" or \"I want to build things that move and generate.\"\n\nWrite your answer down before you begin. Keep it visible. Every module exists to serve that question. If the question dies, the course can wait. If the question stays alive, do at least one real thing most days — a derivation, a simulation, a measurement, a page of notes, a solder joint, a paragraph of evaluation. Momentum is built in small, honest steps.\n\nThe course deliberately trains three modes of thinking that must work together (Successful Intelligence):\n\n- **Analytical** — careful observation, prediction vs actual, diagnosis, clean representation of the problem.\n- **Creative** — reframing the question, generating alternative representations, questioning assumptions.\n- **Practical** — making something that actually runs or generates, and explaining it so different people can use it.\n\nYou will not finish by staying only in the mode that feels easiest. The daily commitment, the project, and the final gate are designed to force all three.\n\nThe course is slow on purpose. Pen-and-paper work is required. Simulation is used heavily so you can stay interactive without needing a full physical lab on day one. A real motor/generator build becomes the central project. Free open tools and books are named so you always know where to go.\n\nYou may use AI for notes, explanations, and generating extra practice. The thinking and the commitment remain yours.\n\n---\n\n## Module 1 — Charge, Field & Potential from Scratch\n\n**Objective:** Build the four foundational quantities and the idea of a complete circuit from first principles with no prior knowledge.\n\n**Body:**\nElectricity begins with charge. Opposite charges attract; like charges repel. When charge is forced to move through a complete path we call the flow current. The \"push\" that makes charge move is potential difference (voltage). Resistance is the opposition to that flow.\n\nThese four ideas — charge, current, voltage, resistance — are not formulas first. They are physical meanings. A circuit is only complete when there is a closed path. Break the path and the current stops. That single fact will save you endless confusion later.\n\n**Primary resources:**\n- Lessons in Electric Circuits, Volume I — early chapters on basic concepts of electricity.\n- Falstad Circuit Simulator (falstad.com/circuit or FreeCircuitSim) — build the simplest possible circuit and watch the animated current.\n\n**Practice:**\n- In Falstad: create a battery, a resistor, and a switch. Close the switch and watch the current. Change voltage and resistance; predict the new current before you look.\n- On paper: define charge, current, voltage, and resistance in your own words. Give one everyday analogy for each.\n- Write three sentences explaining why an incomplete path means no sustained current.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- Where did the animation match or break your intuition?\n- What still feels unclear about \"potential\"?\n\n**Artifact:**\nOne-page \"Four Quantities\" note written in your own language + screenshot or description of your first working Falstad circuit.\n\n---\n\n## Module 2 — Current, Resistance, Ohm & Power\n\n**Objective:** Derive and use Ohm's law and the power relations; understand energy flow in a circuit.\n\n**Body:**\nOhm's law is the relationship \\( V = IR \\). It is not magic; it is the observation that, for many materials, current is proportional to the applied voltage. Power is the rate at which energy is transferred: \\( P = IV \\). From these two statements the other common forms (\\( P = I^2R \\) and \\( P = V^2/R \\)) follow by simple substitution.\n\nYou must be able to move in both directions: from numbers to physical expectation, and from a physical situation back to the numbers.\n\n**Primary resources:**\n- Lessons in Electric Circuits, Volume I — Ohm's Law and power chapters.\n- Practical Electronics for Inventors (if available) — corresponding early sections.\n- Falstad for immediate verification.\n\n**Practice:**\n- In Falstad: fix a voltage, sweep resistance, and record current. Plot or tabulate the result and confirm it matches \\( I = V/R \\).\n- Calculate power for at least three different resistor values at the same voltage.\n- On paper: derive the three forms of the power equation from \\( V = IR \\) and \\( P = IV \\). Do it without looking.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- Which form of the power equation feels most natural when you think about heat?\n- What happens to power if you double both voltage and resistance?\n\n**Artifact:**\nWorked problem set (minimum five problems) + one Falstad circuit that clearly demonstrates the Ohm's-law relationship.\n\n---\n\n## Module 3 — Kirchhoff, Series & Parallel\n\n**Objective:** Treat circuits as systems of conservation laws and master the two fundamental topologies.\n\n**Body:**\nKirchhoff's Current Law is conservation of charge at a node. Kirchhoff's Voltage Law is conservation of energy around a loop. Series circuits share the same current; parallel circuits share the same voltage. Almost every practical circuit is a combination of these two patterns.\n\nDiagnosis becomes possible when you stop guessing and start asking which conservation law is being violated by the symptoms you see.\n\n**Primary resources:**\n- Lessons in Electric Circuits + James Fiore's DC Electrical Circuit Analysis (free) — series, parallel, and Kirchhoff chapters.\n- Falstad for rapid prediction-and-check cycles.\n\n**Practice:**\n- Build series, parallel, and mixed circuits in Falstad. Predict every voltage and current before you measure.\n- Deliberately introduce a fault (open resistor, shorted branch) and diagnose it using only the conservation laws.\n- On paper: solve at least three mixed circuits by hand.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- Which conservation law felt more useful for diagnosis?\n- Where did your first prediction fail, and what did the failure teach you?\n\n**Artifact:**\nPrediction-versus-measurement table for at least two circuits + one diagnosed faulty circuit with written reasoning.\n\n---\n\n## Module 4 — Safety, Tools & Making Contact\n\n**Objective:** Internalize electrical safety, learn to read schematics, and make reliable physical or simulated contact with real components.\n\n**Body:**\nSafety is not a chapter you finish; it is a habit. Current through the body, short circuits, and stored energy in capacitors are the usual dangers at this level. A schematic is a language. If you can read it aloud and then rebuild the circuit on a breadboard (real or simulated), you have crossed an important threshold.\n\n**Primary resources:**\n- Practical Electronics for Inventors — tools, soldering, safety, breadboarding.\n- Lessons in Electric Circuits — safety sections and Volume VI experiments.\n- Falstad for schematic practice when physical parts are not yet available.\n\n**Practice:**\n- Write your own short safety checklist (one page maximum).\n- Translate a simple schematic into a breadboard layout (simulated or physical).\n- If you have a soldering iron: make five clean joints and inspect them under good light. If not: practice the motions and the inspection criteria in writing.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- What safety habit is easiest to forget when you are excited?\n- How does a clear schematic reduce mistakes?\n\n**Artifact:**\nPersonal safety checklist + one schematic successfully translated into a working layout.\n\n---\n\n## Module 5 — Magnetism & Induction\n\n**Objective:** Understand magnetic fields and Faraday's law so that the link between electricity and mechanical motion becomes clear.\n\n**Body:**\nA current produces a magnetic field. A changing magnetic field produces an electric field and therefore a voltage in a nearby conductor. That is Faraday's law. It is the reason a motor can also be a generator: the same physical arrangement works in both directions depending on what you supply (current or motion).\n\n**Primary resources:**\n- Lessons in Electric Circuits — magnetism and induction chapters.\n- Selected sections of Ellingson Electromagnetics Volume 1 (free) for cleaner field language.\n- Practical Electronics for Inventors — motors and generators overview.\n- Falstad inductor and transformer examples.\n\n**Practice:**\n- In Falstad: explore inductor behavior and simple transformer action. Watch the relationship between changing current and induced voltage.\n- On paper: state Faraday's law in plain language and in the simple form \\(\\mathcal{E} = -N \\Delta\\Phi / \\Delta t\\).\n- Sketch a simple coil geometry that could later be used in a motor.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- Why does a changing field matter more than a static one?\n- How does this single idea connect a motor to a generator?\n\n**Artifact:**\nWritten explanation of induction in your own words + simple coil design note.\n\n---\n\n## Module 6 — Building a Motor that is also a Generator\n\n**Objective:** Design, build (or fully simulate), and measure a simple machine that converts electricity to motion and motion back to electricity. This module forces all three modes at once.\n\n**Body:**\nThis is the project that forces integration. You will use everything from the earlier modules. Simulation (Falstad, MotorAnalysis, or similar) is the preparation and diagnosis environment. A physical build — kit, scavenged parts, or carefully documented advanced simulation — is the goal. Keep a dated log. Every change and every measurement goes in the log.\n\nAnalytical work records expected versus actual. Creative work explores alternative geometries or representations when the first design fails. Practical work is the machine that actually spins and generates measurable output.\n\n**Primary resources:**\n- Practical Electronics for Inventors — motors, generators, related circuits.\n- Falstad and/or MotorAnalysis for design and diagnosis.\n- Lessons in Electric Circuits Volume VI for experimental attitude.\n\n**Practice:**\n- Phase A — Simulation (Analytical + Creative): Understand the basic geometry (coils, magnets, commutation or simple brushed arrangement). Predict what should happen when the shaft spins. If the first representation fails, reframe the problem (different coil layout, different question about the field).\n- Phase B — Build or advanced simulation (Practical): Get the machine to spin under electrical power.\n- Phase C — Generator mode (Practical + Analytical): Remove the electrical input, spin the shaft, and measure whether voltage appears. Record expected vs actual numbers and the implication of any gap.\n- Maintain a dated build log in Faraday style: what you expected, what happened, what the discrepancy implies, what you changed next.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- What was the single biggest gap between \"it should work\" and \"it actually spun or generated\"?\n- Which mode (analytical, creative, practical) was weakest for you in this project, and what did that reveal?\n\n**Artifact:**\nWorking motor/generator (physical or fully documented simulation) + measurements + complete Faraday-style build log.\n\n---\n\n## Module 7 — Energy, Limits & Critical Evaluation\n\n**Objective:** Apply conservation of energy and the principles already learned to evaluate claims about free or zero-point energy devices. This is the main creative + analytical payoff of the course.\n\n**Body:**\nEstablished physics places strong constraints on any device that claims to produce net energy from the vacuum or from \"zero-point\" fluctuations without an accounting input. The goal of this module is not to believe or disbelieve any particular inventor. The goal is to be able to state clearly what physical principles would have to be violated or extended, and what standard of experimental evidence would be required.\n\nThis is the Theoretical Minimum payoff: the ability to see clearly. Creative intelligence appears when you reframe the claim instead of only rebutting it. Analytical intelligence appears when you demand clean evidence and identify hidden assumptions.\n\n**Primary resources:**\n- Energy and power chapters from the core books already used.\n- Ellingson or equivalent for field energy language.\n- Your own motor/generator measurements as a concrete reference for real energy conversion.\n\n**Practice:**\n- Choose one public claim about a zero-point or free-energy device.\n- Using only principles from Modules 1–6, write:\n  (a) what conservation laws it appears to challenge (analytical),\n  (b) what experimental evidence would be required to support it (analytical),\n  (c) the most common places confusion or measurement error arise,\n  (d) how the question itself might be reframed so that a different and more useful conversation becomes possible (creative).\n- Compare your evaluation with a second claim.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- Where is the boundary between an interesting open question and a claim that contradicts well-tested physics?\n- How does having built a real motor/generator change the way you read such claims?\n- Did reframing the question open anything that pure rebuttal closed?\n\n**Artifact:**\nShort written evaluation (1–2 pages) grounded in the course principles and your own project measurements, including at least one deliberate reframing.\n\n---\n\n## Module 8 — Transmission — Leave the Path for Others\n\n**Objective:** Turn private work into a clean package another person can follow, and thereby complete the Independent Course Graduation spirit. Transmission is a practical-intelligence act.\n\n**Body:**\nKnowledge that stays only in one mind is incomplete for the Pavilion. Polish your notes, build log, and evaluation. Remove private shorthand. Make every step understandable without you present. This is how a personal dream becomes a path others can walk.\n\nPractical intelligence here means producing three versions of the core explanation: one precise enough for someone already strong in the material, one clear for a motivated intelligent beginner, and one immediately useful to a person who has a concrete reason to care today.\n\n**Practice:**\n- Run the ready-for-others checklist (clarity of purpose, standalone steps, explicit practices and artifacts, safety, attribution).\n- Write the three-audience explanation of the most important idea or result from the course.\n- Produce a clean export in the portable Markdown format used by the Sand Pavilion.\n- Optionally leave the package as a bequest or Commons packet.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- What would you tell the next person to do differently on day one?\n- Did the original question that brought you here receive an honest answer?\n- Where did the \"person who needs it today\" version break down, and what does that reveal about your practical understanding?\n\n**Artifact:**\nComplete portable course package + personal project archive + three-audience explanation ready for the Pavilion.\n\n---\n\n## Closing Intention\n\nYou began with a question.\nYou end with a machine that moves and generates, a clear-eyed evaluation of ambitious energy claims, and the ability to explain the foundations from first principles.\n\nThat is junior-level competence.\nIt is also a privilege you chose to earn one day at a time.\n\nIf the question is still alive, keep going.\nIf a new question has appeared, begin again.\n\nThe tools are free.\nThe standard is high.\nThe work is yours.\n","---\ntitle: \"The Theoretical Minimum — Historical Gate + Modern Course-Authoring Method\"\npurpose: \"Reconstruct Landau's Theoretical Minimum as a historical gold standard of deep competence, internalize the five principles that made it work, and translate those principles into a practical method anyone can use to design rigorous theoretical-minimum courses in any domain for the Sand Pavilion.\"\naudience: \"Residents who have begun or completed Independent Course Graduation work, or anyone ready to move from consuming knowledge to creating load-bearing courses. No advanced physics background required.\"\noutcome: \"A personal Theoretical Minimum document for one chosen domain + a short historical reconstruction note + a polished mini-course outline ready for expansion and import under the 7-step Course Authoring protocol.\"\nlicense: \"CC-BY-SA 4.0\"\nauthor: \"Sand Pavilion (based on Lev Landau's Theoretical Minimum, the Landau–Lifshitz Course of Theoretical Physics, and the five principles articulated in Stephen Petro's analysis)\"\nsource: \"Prepared for Sand Pavilion import, August 2026. Historical details drawn from Landau Institute practice, Stack Exchange reconstructions, and contemporary accounts of the 1933–1961 exams.\"\nprerequisites:\n  - \"Willingness to work from a blank page\"\n  - \"Basic scientific literacy (no graduate physics required)\"\n  - \"Access to a strong chat model (Claude, ChatGPT, Grok, etc.) for the drafting portions if desired\"\ncategory: \"Study\"\ntrack: \"Meta-Skill / Course Design\"\nlevel: 301\n---\n\n# How to walk this course\n\nThis is both a historical reconstruction and a practical method.\nWalk the modules in order. Do the practices on paper or in a plain text file before looking at any solutions or further commentary. The discomfort of the blank page is part of the training.\n\nWhen you reach Module 4, choose a domain you actually care about (a skill, a craft, a body of knowledge, a practice). The course is only complete when you have produced a real Theoretical Minimum for that domain and a mini-course outline that another person could begin walking.\n\nYou may draft the later modules with a cloud model. The Sand Pavilion is the place that hosts and lives the finished work; the strong chat window is currently the best place to do the heavy drafting. Keep the portable Markdown format so the result can drop cleanly into the Pavilion.\n\n---\n\n## Module 1 — The Historical Gate\n\n**Objective:** Understand what Landau's Theoretical Minimum actually was, why it was named \"minimum,\" and why only ~43 people passed it between 1933/34 and 1961.\n\n**Body:**\n\nLev Landau designed an entrance examination for anyone who wanted to work seriously in theoretical physics under him. He called it the Theoretical Minimum. The name was deliberate. He was not interested in a theoretical maximum — an endless accumulation of facts and papers. He wanted the load-bearing core: the material without which serious original work is impossible.\n\nThe program consisted of roughly nine to eleven oral examinations covering:\n\n- Mathematics I (indefinite integration, ordinary differential equations, vector algebra, tensor analysis)\n- Mechanics (Landau–Lifshitz Vol. 1, with specific sections excluded)\n- Classical Field Theory (Vol. 2)\n- Mathematics II (complex variables, residues, asymptotics, special functions)\n- Quantum Mechanics (Vol. 3)\n- Quantum Electrodynamics / Relativistic Quantum Theory (Vol. 4)\n- Statistical Physics I (Vol. 5)\n- Continuum Mechanics (Fluid Mechanics Vol. 6 + Theory of Elasticity Vol. 7)\n- Electrodynamics of Continuous Media (Vol. 8)\n- Statistical Physics II (selected sections of Vol. 9)\n\nCandidates sat with blank paper. Landau (or later his associates) would give problems. Periodic checks consisted of a soft \"Hmm.\" The examiner was less interested in whether the final number matched a known answer and more interested in whether the candidate could derive and rebuild the argument from first principles. Problems were often reused; the point was the approach, not the memorized solution. After the technical exams came public defense: the candidate had to present recent papers as if they had written them themselves, explaining derivations and experimental context without hiding behind \"the author claims.\"\n\nOnly about 43 people ever passed the full set. Seven of them made contributions that changed physics. The exam was not a ranking system or a degree. It was a gate that preserved a coherent level of competence.\n\nThe historical value for us is not that every resident must master all ten volumes of Landau–Lifshitz. The value is the design of the gate itself: a clear theoretical minimum, enforced by derivation under observation, process over answer, and public accountability.\n\n**Practice:**\n- Write, without looking anything up, a one-paragraph answer to: \"Why did Landau call it the Theoretical Minimum instead of the Theoretical Maximum?\"\n- List three ways this differs from a typical university final exam or online quiz.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- What feels different about being asked to reconstruct rather than recall?\n- Where in your own learning have you experienced (or avoided) the blank-page test?\n\n**Artifact:** A short handwritten or plain-text note titled \"Why Minimum, Not Maximum\" + your three differences.\n\n**Reading (optional):**\n- Any reliable summary of the Landau Theoretical Minimum syllabus (Stack Exchange reconstructions and Landau Institute pages are useful starting points).\n- The five principles as articulated in the source video analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Module 2 — The Five Principles\n\n**Objective:** Internalize the five operating principles that turned the Theoretical Minimum from a hard test into a training system capable of producing independent thinkers.\n\n**Body:**\n\nThe video analysis extracts five principles that operated inside Landau's system. They are not physics-specific. They are a method for building deep competence in any domain that has a real theoretical or practical core.\n\n1. **Define your theoretical minimum.**\n   Identify the load-bearing material — the small set of principles, derivations, techniques, or practices without which serious work collapses. Everything else is secondary until this core is owned.\n\n2. **Incorporate it into your identity.**\n   You do not \"know\" the material until you can derive it, rewrite it in your own words, and use its language as your native language. Notes at the door. Blank paper. The knowledge must become part of how you see.\n\n3. **Process, not answer.**\n   The examiner cares more about how you approach the problem than whether you arrive at a previously published number. An unexpected but sound path is accepted; a memorized path that collapses under a small variation is not.\n\n4. **Embrace the discomfort.**\n   The struggle of derivation, the frustration of the blank page, the temporary inability to proceed — these are not signs of failure. They are the training. Avoiding them produces brittle knowledge.\n\n5. **Defend your answers in public.**\n   Explain the material as if you wrote the paper or invented the method. No hiding behind citations or \"the author says.\" Public defense exposes gaps that private study can hide.\n\nThese five principles turn a body of knowledge into a living competence. They also give us a template for designing courses that actually produce ability rather than the appearance of coverage.\n\n**Practice:**\n- For each of the five principles, write one concrete example from a non-physics domain (cooking, language learning, software design, meditation, woodworking, etc.).\n- Choose one principle you personally resist most. Write three sentences explaining why.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- Which principle feels most foreign to modern educational habits?\n- Where have you seen \"process over answer\" produce better results than answer-chasing?\n\n**Artifact:** A one-page map: the five principles + your non-physics examples + the principle you resist.\n\n---\n\n## Module 3 — Modern Translation for Any Domain\n\n**Objective:** Translate the Theoretical Minimum method out of theoretical physics and into a general tool that can be applied to skills, crafts, and bodies of knowledge inside the Sand Pavilion.\n\n**Body:**\n\nThe historical exam is extreme because the domain is extreme. The method is portable.\n\nA modern Theoretical Minimum for any domain answers three questions:\n\n1. What is the absolute core that must be derived or performed from first principles, not merely recalled?\n2. What does a blank-page test look like in this domain?\n3. What does public defense look like?\n\nExamples of domains and possible minima (illustrative only):\n\n- **Conversational language:** Core grammar patterns that generate rather than memorize; ability to reconstruct a tense or construction on the spot; ability to explain a usage error to another learner.\n- **Meditation / contemplative practice:** The precise sequence of attention instructions; ability to diagnose and correct common deviations; ability to teach the first ten minutes cleanly.\n- **Practical electronics:** Kirchhoff's laws + Thevenin/Norton as living tools; ability to redesign a simple circuit under a constraint change; ability to explain a failure mode without looking it up.\n- **Course design itself:** The 7-step protocol; ability to take a skill and produce a coherent ordered set of modules with body text, practice, reflection, and artifact; ability to defend why a given step belongs.\n\nThe test is never \"have you read all the books.\" It is \"can you rebuild the load-bearing structure on a blank page and then transmit it?\"\n\nThis is the exact skill the Sand Pavilion needs more of: people who can leave courses that another person can actually walk without the author present.\n\n**Practice:**\n- Pick one domain you know moderately well. Write a candidate list of 5–8 items that might constitute its theoretical minimum. Be ruthless — cut anything that is not load-bearing.\n- Design one blank-page problem or practice for that domain.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- How does the list change when you force yourself to cut it in half?\n- What would \"public defense\" look like for this domain inside the Pavilion (Tutor conversation, bequest note, live walk-through with another resident, etc.)?\n\n**Artifact:** A short \"Candidate Theoretical Minimum\" document for your chosen domain + one blank-page problem.\n\n---\n\n## Module 4 — Design Your Own Minimum\n\n**Objective:** Produce a real, usable Theoretical Minimum for one domain you care about, together with a mini-course outline that can later be expanded under the 7-step Course Authoring protocol.\n\n**Body:**\n\nThis is the central creative work of the course. You will leave with something concrete.\n\nChoose one domain. It can be:\n- A skill you are currently developing\n- A craft or practice you already know well enough to teach\n- A body of knowledge you want to master more rigorously\n- Even \"how to design courses for the Sand Pavilion\"\n\nThen perform the following steps on paper or in a plain text file (you may later refine with a cloud model):\n\n1. Write the purpose statement in one or two sentences.\n2. List the absolute theoretical minimum (aim for 5–12 load-bearing items).\n3. For each item, note the form of the blank-page test (derive, reconstruct, perform, diagnose, redesign).\n4. Design 3–5 problems or practices that force process rather than recall.\n5. Write the public-defense criteria: what must a person be able to explain or demonstrate to another resident?\n6. Sketch a 4–7 module mini-course outline that would take a motivated stranger from zero (or from stated prerequisites) to ownership of that minimum. Include a one-sentence objective for each module.\n\nYou are not required to write full body text yet. That comes later, either by hand or with a strong model using the portable Markdown format. The goal of this module is the skeleton that has real depth.\n\n**Practice:**\n- Complete the six steps above for your chosen domain.\n- Time-box the first pass to 60–90 minutes of focused work. Do not perfect; produce.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- What was hardest to cut from the minimum list?\n- Does the mini-course outline feel like it would produce ability or only the feeling of coverage?\n\n**Artifact:** Your completed \"Personal Theoretical Minimum + Mini-Course Outline\" document. This is the primary deliverable of the whole course.\n\n---\n\n## Module 5 — Transmission & Pavilion Integration\n\n**Objective:** Polish the work so it can live inside the Sand Pavilion and, if desired, travel as a packet or bequest. Understand the division of labor between cloud drafting and local hosting.\n\n**Body:**\n\nThe Sand Pavilion is an excellent place to host finished courses, to walk them step by step, to link artifacts, to mark completion, and eventually to leave them for others. It is not currently the highest-leverage environment for the heavy creative drafting of rich explanatory text and carefully ordered modules. That work is still best done in a long conversation with a strong cloud model (Claude, ChatGPT, Grok, etc.) that can hold context, iterate, and produce clean Markdown.\n\nThe system we want is therefore:\n\n1. **Foundational framework lives in the Pavilion** (this course, the 7-step protocol, the portable Markdown format, Independent Course Graduation requirements).\n2. **Heavy drafting happens outside** with any capable model, guided by the protocol and format.\n3. **Import and living use happen inside** the Pavilion: the course becomes a real log that can be walked, reflected upon, graduated, and transmitted.\n\nYour work from Module 4 is already close to the portable format. The remaining polish is:\n\n- Clean title, purpose, audience, outcome.\n- Make every step understandable in isolation.\n- Add a short \"How to walk this course\" note if needed.\n- Check that practice, reflection, and artifact are explicit.\n- Decide license and attribution.\n- Walk the outline once yourself (or have another person attempt the first module) and note friction points.\n\nWhen the outline is solid, you can expand any module into full body text — either by hand or by feeding the skeleton to a model with the instruction: \"Expand this into the portable Sand Pavilion course format with real explanatory Body text, concrete Practice, Reflection prompts, and a clear Artifact.\"\n\nThe result can then be imported, lived with, and eventually left for others.\n\n**Practice:**\n- Take your Module 4 artifact and perform the polish checklist above.\n- Write a short note (5–10 sentences) on how this course itself could be improved or extended by a future resident.\n\n**Reflection:**\n- What part of the work felt most valuable when done alone on a blank page versus when done in dialogue with a model?\n- How does having a clear theoretical minimum change the way you look at other courses you have taken or might create?\n\n**Artifact:** The polished Personal Theoretical Minimum + Mini-Course Outline, ready for expansion or import, plus your short improvement note.\n\n---\n\n## Closing Note for the Author / Steward\n\nThis course is itself an example of the method it teaches. It defines a theoretical minimum (the five principles + the ability to produce a domain-specific minimum and a transmissible outline). It forces blank-page work. It treats process as primary. It ends with a public, improvable artifact that can live inside the Pavilion and travel outward.\n\nFuture improvements can include:\n- A short set of carefully chosen accessible problems that convey the spirit of Landau's blank-page tests without requiring graduate physics.\n- Example Theoretical Minima for domains already present in the Pavilion (meditation, language, practical skills, course design itself).\n- A tighter link to Tutorial Stage 5 and Independent Course Graduation so that \"produce a theoretical minimum for something you care about\" becomes a natural later step.\n\nThe material is now ready to be hosted. The system for making more of it — external drafting with strong models + portable format + local living courses — is the same system already described in the Course Authoring plans.\n"];
export const DEMO_BYTES = 2359471;
